```

All code (`main.py`, `natural_mouse/`, `gui/`) lives under `src/` — a
"src layout." `tests/`, `docs/`, and `templates/` are deliberately
separate top-level siblings, not under `src/`. Run the CLI/GUI as
`python src/main.py ...`; run tests as `python -m unittest discover -s
tests -t .` (the `-t .` matters — see `tests/__init__.py`).

## What this project is

A Windows desktop-automation library: finds a UI element on screen via
OpenCV template matching, then moves the real cursor to it along a
human-like curved path (Bezier + easing + jitter) and clicks, or scrolls
the mouse wheel. Built as swappable layers behind abstract base classes
(`PathGenerator`, `InputExecutor`, `ScreenLocator`), composed by
`UIAutomator`. `main.py` (CLI) and `gui/app.py` (Tkinter) are two
independent front ends over that same library.

This is a **manual** tool: every CLI invocation or GUI button press
performs exactly one action and exits/returns to idle. There is no loop,
scheduler, or unattended/background execution anywhere in this codebase,
and none should be added — every action happens because the user
explicitly triggered it.

## Ground rules

- Before answering a question about "the codebase" or making an edit,
  check this file list first. If you need to see a file's actual content,
  read that exact path — do not paraphrase from memory or assume standard
  boilerplate exists (e.g. no setup.py/pyproject.toml exists yet).
- Do not add automation that runs unattended, on a schedule, or in a loop
  without the user explicitly asking for that specific feature by name.
- Follow the existing pattern for new features: abstract base class in
  `base.py`, concrete implementation in its own file, wired together only
  in `automator.py`, `main.py`, or `gui/app.py`.
